@page "/features/database"
@{
    Layout = "_ArticleLayout";
}

<h2 id="two-levels">Two levels of SQL Server insight</h2>
<div class="grid-2">
    <div class="mini">
        <h3>Connections, with no setup</h3>
        <p>Out of the box, IIS Monitor counts the open TCP connections from each app pool's processes to SQL Server: port 1433, any port a local SQL Server listens on, and ports you add. You see them in the <em>DB conns</em> column and charts.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="mini">
        <h3>Load, blocking and slow queries</h3>
        <p>Add a connection string and the Database tab lights up: what each app pool is running in SQL Server right now, what it is waiting for, who is blocking whom, and which queries are slow.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<h2 id="setup">Give IIS Monitor access to SQL Server</h2>
<p>Nothing is installed on SQL Server and no trace is started. IIS Monitor reads SQL Server's dynamic management views, which needs one permission.</p>
<ol class="steps">
    <li>Open <a href="/features/settings">Settings</a> and, in the <strong>SQL Server activity</strong> box under <em>SQL Server connections</em>, add one connection string per SQL Server, for example:
        <pre><code>Server=.;Integrated Security=true;TrustServerCertificate=true</code></pre>
    </li>
    <li>Let that login see server activity. The IIS Monitor service runs as LocalSystem, so for a SQL Server on the same machine, run this once in SQL Server Management Studio:
        <pre><code>USE master;
IF SUSER_ID(N'NT AUTHORITY\SYSTEM') IS NULL CREATE LOGIN [NT AUTHORITY\SYSTEM] FROM WINDOWS;
GRANT VIEW SERVER STATE TO [NT AUTHORITY\SYSTEM];</code></pre>
    </li>
    <li>For a SQL Server on another machine, grant the same permission to the IIS server's computer account, <code>DOMAIN\SERVER$</code>. On SQL Server 2022 and later, <code>VIEW SERVER PERFORMANCE STATE</code> is enough.</li>
</ol>
<p>Until a connection string is set, the Database tab shows these steps. Later they stay available under <span class="ui">How to give IIS Monitor access to SQL Server</span> at the bottom of the tab, and a warning appears there if SQL Server can't be reached or the permission is missing. In standalone mode the dashboard connects as your own Windows account, which then needs the same permission.</p>

<h2 id="how-it-works">How queries are matched to app pools</h2>
<p>Every second (set <span class="ui">Ask SQL Server every</span> from 1 to 10 seconds), the service asks SQL Server which requests are running, through <code>sys.dm_exec_requests</code>, and which client process sent each one (<code>host_process_id</code>). It maps that process ID to the app pool it belongs to, exactly as it does for CPU and memory.</p>
<p>A query counts as coming from this server when the client reports this computer's name as its host name, which is the default. Connection strings that set <code>Workstation ID</code> show up as “other servers”.</p>

<h2 id="load">Database load per app pool</h2>
<p>The line at the top sums it up, for example: <em>Database load over the last minute (average running queries): IIS app pools 6.43 · other programs on this server 0.42 · other servers 1.08 · SQL Server process CPU 23.4 %</em>. The grid below breaks it down by pool, ranked by the last minute:</p>
<dl class="defs">
    <dt>DB load (1 min)</dt>
    <dd>The average number of the pool's queries running at once over the last minute. 1.00 means one query busy all the time; 3.00 means three. Sampling every second makes this accurate over a minute even for queries that take a few milliseconds.</dd>
    <dt>on CPU, waiting</dt>
    <dd>The same load, split into time spent on CPU and time spent waiting: for locks, disk, memory, the network or the app.</dd>
    <dt>now</dt>
    <dd>Queries running at the latest sample.</dd>
    <dt>Blocked now</dt>
    <dd>The pool's queries waiting for another session's locks.</dd>
    <dt>Blocking others</dt>
    <dd>Other sessions waiting for this pool's locks.</dd>
    <dt>Idle in transaction</dt>
    <dd>Sessions holding a transaction open while doing nothing, a common cause of blocking.</dd>
    <dt>Slow running now</dt>
    <dd>Queries already past the slow threshold and still running.</dd>
    <dt>Sessions</dt>
    <dd>The pool's sessions, and how many are active.</dd>
</dl>
<div class="note">
    @Icons.Svg("help")
    <div><p><strong>Why sample instead of using SQL Server's totals?</strong> SQL Server's own per-session counters only update when a query finishes, and reset every time a pooled connection is reused, which in a web app is constantly. Sampling what is running every second gives a steady, comparable number for every pool.</p></div>
</div>
<p>Two charts show <strong>database load by app pool</strong> and <strong>blocked queries by app pool</strong>, one line per pool ticked on the <a href="/features/overview">Overview</a> tab. The <a href="/features/app-pools">App pools</a> grid also has a <em>DB load</em> column, and each pool's <a href="/features/history">history</a> keeps database load, blocking and slow-query counts.</p>

<h2 id="running-now">Running now</h2>
<p>Every query from this server that is running at that moment, longest first. Queries already over the slow threshold are red.</p>
<dl class="defs">
    <dt>App pool / client</dt>
    <dd>The app pool, or the process ID and program name for anything else on the server, such as SQL Server Agent.</dd>
    <dt>Database, Running for, CPU, Reads</dt>
    <dd>Where it runs, for how long so far, CPU time used and logical reads.</dd>
    <dt>Doing</dt>
    <dd>What it is doing, in plain words rather than wait-type names.</dd>
    <dt>Blocked by</dt>
    <dd>The session holding the lock it waits for, with that session's app pool, such as <em>session 87 (Billing)</em>.</dd>
    <dt>Statement</dt>
    <dd>The procedure and statement, with literal values replaced by <code>?</code>.</dd>
</dl>
<p>Some of the descriptions in the <em>Doing</em> column, and what they usually mean:</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Doing</th><th scope="col">Wait type</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Running on CPU</td><td>—</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Waiting for a lock held by another session</td><td><code>LCK_*</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Finished, but the app is reading the results slowly (too many rows, or row-by-row processing)</td><td><code>ASYNC_NETWORK_IO</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Reading data pages from disk (missing index or too little memory)</td><td><code>PAGEIOLATCH_*</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Waiting for the transaction log to be written</td><td><code>WRITELOG</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Waiting for memory to run the query (large sort or hash)</td><td><code>RESOURCE_SEMAPHORE</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Long CPU work, sharing the CPU with other queries</td><td><code>SOS_SCHEDULER_YIELD</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Parallel query waiting for its slowest thread</td><td><code>CXPACKET</code>, <code>CXCONSUMER</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Contention on hot in-memory pages (often tempdb or inserts at the end of a table)</td><td><code>PAGELATCH_*</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Waiting for a linked server or remote call</td><td><code>OLEDB</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Waiting for an availability-group replica to confirm the commit</td><td><code>HADR_SYNC_COMMIT</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<h2 id="slow-queries">Slow queries</h2>
<screenshot name="slow-queries" alt="Slow queries list grouped by stored procedure or batch, with run count, average and longest duration, total time, CPU, reads and the main wait">Slow queries over the last hour, costliest first, with the selected procedure's details below.</screenshot>
<p>Every query that runs longer than the threshold (<strong>2 seconds</strong> by default, set in <a href="/features/settings">Settings</a>) is recorded when it finishes, with its app pool, database, duration, CPU, reads, main wait (or CPU), the app pool that blocked it if any, and its statement. Slow queries are kept with the rest of the history.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Named after what the app sent.</strong> Each slow query is named after the stored procedure the app called, or the batch or parameterized query it sent, even when the time went into a nested procedure, function or trigger. Runs that differ only in literal values group together.</li>
    <li><strong>Costliest first.</strong> The list groups runs and sorts them by total time, with the number of runs, average, longest, total, CPU, reads and what they were mostly doing.</li>
    <li><strong>Where the time went.</strong> Select a row to see the full statement and the module where the longest run spent most of its time, and whether, and by which app pool, it was blocked.</li>
    <li><strong>Filter it.</strong> Show the last 15 minutes, hour, 24 hours or 7 days, for all pools or one.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="accuracy">Accurate, even for queries just over the threshold</h3>
<p>Queries are watched from one sample interval before the threshold, and sampled once more right when they reach it. When a query ends between two samples, its exact duration is taken from SQL Server's record of the session's last request, so queries just over the threshold aren't missed.</p>

<h3 id="ignored">Waits that are ignored on purpose</h3>
<p>Some queries wait by design: Service Broker and <code>SqlDependency</code> listeners, <code>WAITFOR</code> and trace readers. They would otherwise look like endless slow queries, so they are left out.</p>

<h2 id="privacy">No customer data in your history</h2>
<p>Statements are stored and shown with literal values replaced by <code>?</code>, double-quoted values included, so <code>WHERE Email = 'x@y.com'</code> becomes <code>WHERE Email = ?</code>. Customer data never gets copied into the history database.</p>

<h2 id="how-to">How to use it: find what is slowing SQL Server down</h2>
<ol class="steps">
    <li>Look at the pools grid: the pool at the top has the highest database load over the last minute.</li>
    <li>Compare <em>on CPU</em> with <em>waiting</em>. Mostly CPU points to heavy queries; mostly waiting points to locks, disk or the app reading results slowly.</li>
    <li>If <em>Blocked now</em> or <em>Blocking others</em> is above zero, open <strong>Running now</strong>: the <em>Blocked by</em> column names the session and app pool holding the lock. <em>Idle in transaction</em> sessions are a frequent culprit.</li>
    <li>Open <strong>Slow queries</strong> for the last hour or day. The procedure at the top costs the most time in total; select it to see the statement inside it that took longest.</li>
    <li>Use the <a href="/features/history">History</a> tab to check whether that pool's database load changed after a deployment.</li>
</ol>
